---
layout: article
title: Traffic sources
description: How Appwrite Analytics works out where visitors come from, with channels, referring sources, and UTM campaign parameters.
---

The **Traffic sources** card on the dashboard shows where visitors came from in three ways: the type of source as a **channel**, the referring site as a **source**, and the UTM tags on the link they followed as a **campaign**.

Appwrite works these out from two fields of each event: the referrer, which is the page a visitor came from, and the event URL. The Web helper sends the browser's referrer with every pageview. In a single-page app, the browser keeps the original referrer while the visitor moves between routes, so every pageview in the visit keeps its source.

# Channels {% #channels %}

{% only_dark %}
![Traffic sources card on the Channels tab, next to the Humans vs bots card](/images/docs/analytics/dark/traffic-sources.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Traffic sources card on the Channels tab, next to the Humans vs bots card](/images/docs/analytics/traffic-sources.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Every event gets one of seven channels.

- `paid`: the URL has a paid `utm_medium`, such as `cpc`, `ppc`, `paid`, `display`, or `retargeting`, or an ad click ID: `gclid`, `gbraid`, `wbraid`, `msclkid`, or `dclid`.
- `email`: the URL has an email `utm_medium`, such as `email` or `newsletter`, or the referrer is a webmail site such as `mail.google.com` or `outlook.live.com`.
- `social`: the URL has a social `utm_medium`, such as `social` or `social-media`, or the referrer is a social site such as `facebook.com`, `x.com`, `t.co`, `linkedin.com`, `reddit.com`, `youtube.com`, `bsky.app`, or `news.ycombinator.com`.
- `ai`: the referrer is an AI assistant, such as `chatgpt.com`, `claude.ai`, `gemini.google.com`, `perplexity.ai`, `copilot.microsoft.com`, or `chat.deepseek.com`.
- `search`: the URL has `utm_medium` set to `organic` or `search`, or the referrer is a search engine such as Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Yahoo, Ecosia, or Brave Search.
- `direct`: there's no referrer and no recognized `utm_medium`, or the referrer is the same site.
- `referral`: the referrer is any other site.

Appwrite checks `utm_medium` and ad click IDs first, so a tagged link wins over the referrer. Without a referrer, a link tagged with an unrecognized medium, such as `utm_medium=partner`, counts as `direct`.

Among referrers, Appwrite checks webmail first, then AI assistants, then search engines, then social sites. That order keeps `gemini.google.com` in `ai` and `mail.google.com` in `email`, even though both are Google hosts.

The `fbclid` parameter that Facebook adds to shared links doesn't mark a visit as paid, because it appears on unpaid shares too.

## Channels of other events {% #other-events %}

Appwrite works out the channel of each event on its own. The Web helper sends a referrer with pageviews only, so its other events, such as `engagement_time`, `scroll_depth`, and custom events, count as `direct` unless their URL has UTM tags. A visitor who arrived from a search engine then appears under both `search` and `direct`.

To see only where visitors arrived from, add an **Event** filter equal to `pageview`. See [Filter and compare traffic](/docs/products/analytics/filters).

# Sources {% #sources %}

The source is the referrer's hostname, without a leading `www.`, such as `google.com`, `news.ycombinator.com`, or `l.facebook.com`. Subdomains stay separate, so `mail.google.com` and `google.com` are different sources.

A referrer on the same hostname as the event, with or without `www.`, is a visitor moving around your own site. It has no source and counts as `direct`.

# Campaigns {% #campaigns %}

UTM parameters are tags you add to links in newsletters, ads, and posts. Appwrite reads five of them from the event URL:

- `utm_source`: where the link was placed, such as `newsletter` or `twitter`.
- `utm_medium`: the kind of link, such as `email` or `cpc`. It also sets the channel.
- `utm_campaign`: the campaign name, such as `october_launch`.
- `utm_content`: which link or ad variant, such as `header_button`.
- `utm_term`: the search keyword for paid search.

For example, a visitor who opens this link counts toward the `email` channel and the `october_launch` campaign:

```text
https://example.com/pricing?utm_source=newsletter&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=october_launch
```

In the **Traffic sources** card, the **Campaigns** tab has a menu to switch between **UTM campaigns**, **UTM sources**, **UTM mediums**, **UTM contents**, and **UTM terms**. Appwrite stores the page as `/pricing`, without the query string.

# AI assistants and AI bots {% #ai %}

The `ai` channel counts people who followed a link from an AI assistant to your site. When an assistant fetches your pages itself, to answer someone's question, the request comes from a bot instead. Those requests appear in the **Humans vs bots** and **AI agents and crawlers** cards. See [Bots and AI traffic](/docs/products/analytics/bots).
